{
  "result": "I think we got mornings completely wrong. \n\nWe treat them like a launch pad, all countdown and fuel and GO, GO, GO, when they're actually more like a waiting room between two worlds. \n\nIn sleep you were untethered. No name, no bills, no personality to maintain. And then your alarm drags you back into character. The trick is not rushing that part. \n\nThere's about twenty minutes where your brain is still soft, still open-source. Coffee tastes like a decision, not a habit. Light looks like light, not just illumination for your to-do list. You could almost decide to be someone else entirely if you sat with it long enough. \n\nI try to protect that window. No phone. No news. No optimizing. I sit on the edge of my bed and let my apartment come into focus, one object at a time. The chipped mug. The plant that somehow refuses to die. The faint hum of the refrigerator, which is the apartment's way of breathing. \n\nIt's not meditation. It's loitering in your own life. And it changes everything after. The day stops feeling like something that happens to you. It feels like something you walked into, on purpose, with your eyes actually open. \n\nMost people don't need a new life. They just need a wider doorway into the one they already have. Morning is that doorway. Don't sprint through it. Linger. Make some coffee. Let the glitch last a little longer than it should. You'll be surprised what slips through.",
